  "problem": "The tangent line to the eagle's flight path is horizontal at $t=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,\\text{s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $-\\sqrt{2}\\notin (0,\\infty ),$ so we only consider the value $t=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{5}{27}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the curve's tangent is horizontal when $t=\\sqrt{2}.$",
